why_this_one:
It is the whole of the client's Poison, in ZzzCharacter.cpp's damage arm:

    case AT_SKILL_POISON:
    case AT_SKILL_POISON_STR:
        if (o->Type == MODEL_PLAYER)
            CreateEffect(MODEL_POISON, to->Position, o->Angle, o->Light);
        Vector(0.4f, 0.6f, 1.f, Light);

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
            CreateParticle(BITMAP_SMOKE, to->Position, o->Angle, Light, 1);

        if (c->SkillSuccess)
            g_CharacterRegisterBuff(to, eDeBuff_Poison);
        PlayBuffer(SOUND_HEART);
        break;

One cloud, ten puffs of smoke, one wave and a debuff icon on the target.

*The model is the player's alone.* `if (o->Type == MODEL_PLAYER)` guards the CreateEffect and nothing else in the arm, so a monster that poisons somebody makes the ten puffs and no cloud. That is the one place a spell's picture in MU depends on who threw it, and it is the reverse of Energy Ball, whose thirty lines of monster sit above a `default` that is every player.

*Whose angle it is.* `o->Angle` - the caster's facing, not the target's - which is the same reading Ice01.json argues at length. The cloud is square to the man who threw it.

*And the light that is thrown away.* `Vector(0.4f, 0.6f, 1.f, Light)` is computed for the ten particles and never seen: BITMAP_SMOKE's sub-type 1 mover writes `Vector(Luminosity * 0.5f, Luminosity * 1.f, Luminosity * 0.8f, o->Light)` over it on the particle's very first frame. So the puffs are green whatever the create arm says, and the blue-ish vector above them is dead code that reads like an intention. Transcribed as a finding rather than as a colour.
